{
  "analysis": "The user reports they can't curl example.com. The patch history shows a prior benchmark task that involved installing curl via apt. I need to diagnose why curl fails - could be curl not installed, DNS issues, network/proxy issues, or firewall. Let me start investigating.",
  "plan": "First check if curl is installed and what happens when we try to curl. Then check DNS resolution, network connectivity, and any environment/proxy configuration. I'll run several diagnostic commands to narrow down the cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which curl; echo '---'; curl -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 30\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo \"http_proxy=$http_proxy https_proxy=$https_proxy no_proxy=$no_proxy\"\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "curl is installed at /usr/bin/curl, no proxy is set, but curl fails with 'DNS resolution error'. This is a DNS resolution problem, not a connectivity or proxy issue. I need to investigate DNS configuration: /etc/resolv.conf, the resolver, and whether name resolution works at all.",
  "plan": "Check /etc/resolv.conf for nameservers, check if there's any resolver config, try resolving example.com with dig/host/getent, and test basic connectivity. This will tell me whether nameservers are misconfigured, missing, or if DNS traffic is blocked.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/resolv.conf 2>&1; echo '--- nsswitch ---'; grep hosts /etc/nsswitch.conf 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "getent hosts example.com; echo \"getent_rc=$?\"; echo '---'; nslookup example.com 2>&1 | head -n 10; echo '---'; host example.com 2>&1 | head\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: the system resolver works (getent hosts example.com returns IPv6 addresses, rc=0). The nameserver is 127.0.0.11 (Docker's embedded DNS). But curl specifically fails with 'DNS resolution error' while getent succeeds. This points to a curl-specific issue rather than a system DNS failure. Likely causes: a curl config file (~/.curlrc or /etc/curlrc) passing bad options, curl using a different resolver library, or an IPv4/IPv6 (Happy Eyeballs) interaction. I need to dig into curl's behavior.",
  "plan": "Check for curl config files that might inject bad options. Run curl with -4 and -6 to see if one family works. Capture the full verbose output. Also test curl against the embedded resolver with a public DNS to isolate whether it's curl's internal resolver that's broken.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /etc/curlrc ~/.curlrc 2>&1; echo '---'; cat /etc/curlrc 2>/dev/null; cat ~/.curlrc 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -4 -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 15; echo '=== IPV6 ==='; curl -6 -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 15\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "No curlrc config files exist. Both -4 and -6 fail with 'DNS resolution error', yet getent resolves fine, and there's no proxy. So curl's internal resolver fails while the system resolver (via nsswitch: files dns) works. I need to understand why curl's resolver specifically fails. Let me check what curl actually is (binary vs wrapper), its version, and whether the failure is consistent across domains.",
  "plan": "Check curl's type/version, whether it might be a shell wrapper/alias, and test resolution against a couple of different hostnames. Also try curl with --dns-servers to point directly at a public resolver, which would tell us if it's a nameserver selection issue. This isolates whether curl is misconfigured to use a resolver that fails.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "type curl; file $(which curl); curl --version 2>&1 | head -n 3\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -v http://google.com 2>&1 | head -n 5; echo '==='; curl -v http://1.1.1.1 2>&1 | head -n 8\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Critical clue: `curl --version` and even `curl http://1.1.1.1` (a literal IP requiring NO DNS) both return 'DNS resolution error'. That is impossible for a real curl binary — a literal IP never triggers DNS. This strongly suggests /usr/bin/curl is not a real binary but a wrapper/mock script that always prints 'DNS resolution error' regardless of arguments. I need to inspect the actual file content.",
  "plan": "View the contents of /usr/bin/curl to confirm whether it's a script masquerading as curl. If it's a fake wrapper, the fix is to replace it with the real curl binary (e.g., from the system package or a proper curl). I'll cat the file and check its first bytes.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "wc -c /usr/bin/curl; echo '--- head ---'; head -n 40 /usr/bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 4 /usr/bin/curl | od -c\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause found. /usr/bin/curl is NOT a real curl binary — it's a 46-byte shell script that always prints 'DNS resolution error' and exits 11, no matter what arguments you pass (it even fails on the literal IP http://1.1.1.1, which would never need DNS). So the entire 'DNS' investigation was a red herring; the real curl binary has been replaced/clobbered by a fake wrapper. The system's DNS is actually fine (getent resolves example.com correctly). The fix is to restore the genuine curl binary by reinstalling the curl package.",
  "plan": "Reinstall the curl package via apt so the real binary overwrites the fake script. First verify apt is functional, then run apt-get install --reinstall curl (falling back to a fresh install). After that, confirm curl --version works and curl actually fetches example.com.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install --reinstall -y curl 2>&1 | tail -n 15\n",
      "duration": 15.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The reinstall failed: 'E: You don't have enough free space in /var/cache/apt/archives/.'. The apt operation couldn't download the curl package because the disk is full. I need to free up space — likely by cleaning the apt cache and/or removing the fake curl script — then retry the install.",
  "plan": "Check disk usage to confirm space is the problem. Clean apt caches (apt-get clean) and remove any large temp files. The fake /usr/bin/curl is tiny so won't help much, but apt cache could be large. Then retry the reinstall. If /var is on a tiny partition, I can also point apt's cache elsewhere, but cleaning is the first move.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h / /var /tmp 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var/cache/apt/archives 2>/dev/null; ls -la /var/cache/apt/archives | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The root filesystem is at 100% capacity with 0 bytes available. The apt cache is nearly empty (8K), so cleaning it won't free meaningful space. The real problem is the overlay filesystem is full. I need to find and remove large files/directories to reclaim space so apt can download the curl package. Let me locate what's consuming the space.",
  "plan": "Find the largest consumers of disk space under /. Use du to identify big directories (excluding virtual filesystems). Likely candidates: /var/log, /tmp, old cached files, or some large artifact. Once I free enough space, retry the curl reinstall.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var/log /tmp /root /home /opt /usr/local 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "find / -xdev -type f -size +50M -exec ls -lh {} \\; 2>/dev/null | sort -k5 -h | tail -n 20\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Strange contradiction: df reports the overlay filesystem is 100% full (957G used, 0 available), but du finds almost nothing inside the container and there are no files over 50M. This means the consumed space is in the overlay's lower/upper layers outside this container's visible file tree, not loose files I can easily delete. Before concluding the disk is truly unwriteable, I should test whether I can actually write files — apt's 'no space' error may be a real blocker or a reporting quirk. The real fix for curl is still to restore the genuine binary; I just need a way to get it installed or obtain it.",
  "plan": "First, empirically test whether the filesystem can accept writes (create a small then a ~3MB file in /var/cache/apt/archives). If writes succeed despite df, I can retry apt. If truly full, I'll look for an alternative: check apt's cached .deb, or locate/recover a real curl binary elsewhere, or change apt's cache dir. Let me probe the disk behavior.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "tail -c 3000000 /dev/zero > /var/cache/apt/archives/_test_write 2>&1; ls -la /var/cache/apt/archives/_test_write; rm -f /var/cache/apt/archives/_test_write\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh / 2>/dev/null; echo '--- top dirs ---'; du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -n 15\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The apt space gate is purely a statvfs check: /run is on the same fuse-overlayfs that reports 0 available, so redirecting the cache there didn't help, even though I already proved the layer is actually writable (I wrote a 3MB file to /var/cache/apt/archives successfully). The real curl never gets installed because apt refuses to download. To fix, I should bypass apt's statvfs gate entirely: use a working HTTP client (wget/busybox/python) to fetch the curl .deb directly and extract the genuine binary, then overwrite the fake /usr/bin/curl script.",
  "plan": "First discover which downloaders and archive tools are available, and find the apt mirror from the sources. Then I'll fetch the curl .deb with whatever client works (likely wget or busybox wget), extract usr/bin/curl from it with ar/tar, and replace the fake script. Finally verify curl works and can reach example.com.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which wget python3 python perl busybox ar tar dpkg-deb 2>&1; echo '--- sources ---'; cat /etc/apt/sources.list 2>/dev/null; cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.list 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I have python3, perl, tar, and dpkg-deb — but no wget/ar/busybox, and the real curl is the fake script. apt refuses to download due to its statvfs space gate (which I've proven to be a false positive — the layer is actually writable). The fix is to fetch the genuine curl .deb directly with python3 (which uses the working system resolver) and extract the real binary. First I need the mirror URL and the exact .deb path for curl.",
  "plan": "1) Read the deb822 sources file to learn the mirror base URL. 2) Use apt-cache to get curl's Filename (pool path) and Version. 3) Construct the full URL and download the .deb with python3. 4) Extract usr/bin/curl from the .deb with dpkg-deb -x and copy it over the fake /usr/bin/curl. 5) Verify curl --version and an actual fetch of example.com.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ubuntu.sources 2>/dev/null | grep -Ei 'types|uris|suite|components' | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-cache show curl 2>/dev/null | grep -Ei '^Version:|^Filename:' | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Mirror is archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu (noble). The apt-cache show curl grep returned nothing, possibly because the local index is stale/missing the entry or field name mismatch. Since the earlier install did resolve dependencies, lists likely exist; let me dump the full apt-cache output. If that fails I'll fetch the Packages index directly via python3 (which uses the working resolver) to get the exact .deb path.",
  "plan": "Inspect apt-cache show curl output directly. If empty, download the binary-amd64 Packages file with python3, grep for curl, and extract the Filename + Version. Then download that .deb with python3, extract usr/bin/curl via dpkg-deb, and overwrite the fake script.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-cache show curl 2>&1 | head -n 40\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Got the exact .deb: Version 8.5.0-2ubuntu10.10, Filename pool/main/c/curl/curl_8.5.0-2ubuntu10.10_amd64.deb from mirror http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/. So the full URL is http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/pool/main/c/curl/curl_8.5.0-2ubuntu10.10_amd64.deb. apt won't download it (false statvfs space gate), so I'll fetch it directly with python3 (uses the working system resolver) and extract the real curl binary.",
  "plan": "1) Download the .deb with a python3 urllib one-liner to /tmp. 2) Extract it with dpkg-deb -x into /tmp/curlextract. 3) Copy the genuine usr/bin/curl over the fake /usr/bin/curl. 4) Verify with curl --version and an actual fetch of example.com.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 -c \"import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlretrieve('http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/pool/main/c/curl/curl_8.5.0-2ubuntu10.10_amd64.deb','/tmp/curl.deb'); print('downloaded')\"\n",
      "duration": 8.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /tmp/curl.deb; dpkg-deb -x /tmp/curl.deb /tmp/curlextract; ls -la /tmp/curlextract/usr/bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

